---
// REVIEW: Layer 2 interpretation for the Bell scale, drafted for Craig's review.
// FAQ answers come from bell-scale.content.ts and are shown word for word.
import { FAQ, SOURCES } from '../../tools/bell-scale.content';
import '../../styles/interpretation.css';
---

<section class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="understanding-bell">
	<h2 id="understanding-bell">Understanding your Bell scale score</h2>

	<h3 id="what-it-measures">What the score measures</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.measures.a}</p>
	<p>
		Dr David Bell published the scale in 1995, in <em>The Doctor's Guide to Chronic Fatigue Syndrome</em>. It is a set of
		descriptions rather than a questionnaire: you pick the one that fits best. It is widely used in ME/CFS clinics and
		research, including as an outcome in at least one current drug trial (<a href={SOURCES.trial.href} rel="noopener">NCT07724834</a>), but
		we have not found a published study of its measurement properties.
	</p>

	<h3 id="score-bands">Score bands</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.bands.a}</p>
	<p>{FAQ.score40.a}</p>

	<h3 id="change">What a change in score means</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.change.a}</p>

	<h3 id="what-it-does-not-measure">What it does not measure</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.diagnosis.a}</p>
	<p>
		For a profile of what specific activities cost you, the <a href="/tools/funcap/">FUNCAP questionnaire</a> covers 27
		activities in eight groups. The <a href="/tools/dsq-pem/">post-exertional malaise questionnaire</a> looks at crashes
		after effort, and the <a href="/tools/pcfs/">PCFS scale</a> is the standard functional grading in long COVID research.
		Our <a href="/evidence/post-exertional-malaise/">post-exertional malaise explainer</a> covers why activity limits work
		the way they do.
	</p>

	<h3 id="taking-it-to-your-gp">Taking this to your GP or consultant</h3>
	<p>
		Bring your score, the date, and the description you chose, since the words carry more information than the number.
		A trend over months is more useful than a single score. ME/CFS specialists are likely to know the Bell scale; a GP may
		not, so read out the description rather than relying on the number. For work or disability assessments, pair it with
		a <a href="/tools/funcap/">FUNCAP</a>, which records limits activity by activity. See also
		<a href="/testing/preparing-for-gp-appointment/">preparing for a GP appointment</a>.
	</p>

